description:
'''Pontic Greek music''', also called '''Pontian Greek music''', comprises the musical traditions of the [[Pontic Greeks]] from antiquity to the modern day. Song and dance have a long history in the [[Pontus (region)|Pontos]], ranging from ancient dances to the [[Acritic songs]] to folk songs. Certain dances date to ancient times, such as the [[pyrrhichios]]. Pontian music has evolved alongside [[Pontic Greek folk dance|Pontic dance]].

Acritic songs, which describe folk heroes such as [[Digenes Akritas]] who lived and fought on the borders of the empire in [[Byzantine Empire|Byzantine times]], date to the medieval era. These songs were also sung in other parts of the Byzantine empire.{{sfn|Kazhdan|Talbot|Cutler|Gregory|1991|p=48}}{{sfn|Kazhdan|Talbot|Cutler|Gregory|1991|p=622}} Versions of the Acritic songs exist today, accompanied by modern Pontic instruments and singing techniques. More recent folk songs include love songs, mourning songs, and war songs. These date to the late 1800s or earlier during [[Ottoman Empire|Ottoman times]].{{sfn|Michailidis|2016|pp=81-82}} After the [[Greek genocide]] and [[Greek-Turkish population exchange|1923 population exchange]] pushed most Pontians out of their traditional homeland, Pontians wrote new folk songs about their experiences in the diaspora. In the 1970s, the Neopontic genre emerged, combining traditional Pontian folk music with contemporary Greek popular music.{{sfn|Tsekouras|2016|pp=137-138}}

Traditional instruments used in the 21st century include the [[kemençe of the Black Sea|lyra]], [[davul|daouli]], [[zurna|zourna]], [[dankiyo]], [[Tulum (bagpipe)|touloum]], and [[oud]]. Pontian folk music often incorporates [[polyphony]], which is the simultaneous use of multiple [[melody|melodies]]. Pontic music is structured in [[hexachord]]s and typically has a rapid tempo. [[Additive rhythm and divisive rhythm|Asymmetric rhythms]] are common. Instrumental music may be accompanied by vocals, and singers typically use [[vibrato]]. Some songs are set up in a [[call-and-response]] style, with a lead singer and a chorus. The 5/8 rhythm is typical of modern Pontic music.{{sfn|Tsekouras|2016|p=3}}

Pontic Greek music has been influenced by the various cultures present in the Black Sea region throughout history, especially [[Laz people#Music|Laz music]].
